    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i'm working on a low-fat mayo prototype and the emulsion is breaking pretty quick when we drop the pH. could you explain the molecular mechanism of how lecithin stabilizes the interface, specifically in high-acid environments?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Lecithin, a phospholipid, contributes to the stability of emulsions like low-fat mayonnaise by forming a protective interfacial layer. In high-acid environments, the lecithin molecules have hydrophilic (water-attracting) phosphate heads and hydrophobic (oil-attracting) fatty acid tails. When the pH increases, the negative charge on the heads can more effectively attract water, reducing the surface tension and allowing the oil droplets to be dispersed throughout the water. The amphiphilic nature of lecithin helps to prevent the droplets from coalescing, keeping the emulsion stable despite pH fluctuations.",
      "source": "target",
