    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. but the lecithin isn't doing enough to stop the oil from separating during the agitation phase. i'm seeing lots of tiny droplets coalescing into larger ones almost immediately once the acid is added. is there more to the electrostatic repulsion aspect than just the charge on the heads, or am I missing a thermodynamic component here?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Electrostatic repulsion is indeed a major factor in the stability of lecithin-stabilized emulsions, especially in high-acid environments where the lecithin phosphate headgroups have an increased negative charge. This repulsion prevents the oil droplets from coming into close contact and coalescing. However, the thermodynamics of emulsification also play a crucial role; cream of tartar (citric acid) is sometimes used as an acidic food additive to adjust the pH of mayonnaise.\n\n\nConsequently, the addition of an acid can impact the overall energy balance within the emulsion. An acidic environment can increase the dissociation of charged groups on the lecithin molecules, potentially leading to a higher electrostatic repulsion force. Yet, because the electrostatic forces are entropically unfavorable, their effect is often negated by the total number of interactions. On the other hand, the release of a proton (H+) from citric acid can lead to stronger binding with the negatively charged phospholipid headgroups, thus affecting the electrostatic repulsion.\n\n\nAdditionally, the amide group in the glycerol backbone of the lecithin molecule can form hydrogen bonds with water, hence forming a thicker microemulsion layer around the oil droplets.",
      "source": "target",
