    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense, but the hydrogen bonding at the glycerol backbone doesn't seem to be providing enough steric hindrance to counteract the droplet collision during high-shear agitation. if we're looking at the thermodynamic stability, is it possible that we're seeing a spontaneous reduction in interfacial tension that's actually driving the coalescence rather than preventing it? i'm trying to figure out if we need to introduce a different surfactant or if there's a way to manipulate the ionic strength to stabilize the interface better.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
